  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...After a dry and increasingly sunny Monday low clouds \nand rain showers return Tuesday/Tuesday night with a rumble of \nthunder possible.  \n\nSatellite shows the primary low impacting southern New England \nspinning south of the islands this morning as it makes its way east. \nRain has already come to an end in parts of western MA as the mid \nlevel dry slot moves in and this will continue to spread east, \nshutting off precipitation from west to east by sunrise. A drier \natmospheric column makes a brief appearance overhead today as a \ntransient mid level ridge moves through. Thus, can expect clearing \nskies today with plenty of sun, especially in the afternoon. This \nridging also brings a push of warmer air; 925 mb temps rising into \nthe 14-16C range together with full sun should make for high \ntemperatures 10-15 degrees warmer than Sunday, in the low 70s. \n\nMonday night things change as a low center over the Great Lakes \napproaches while high pressure sits over Nova Scotia. This initially \nturns surface flow out of the northeast advecting cooler, saturated \nair onshore making for low clouds with patchy drizzle to start \nTuesday. This brings highs back into the 60s. The warm front lifts \nnorth Tuesday afternoon bringing scattered rain showers which \ncontinue until the cold front drops through sometime Wednesday. SNE \nwill be socked in with clouds all of Tuesday and Tuesday night as \nmoisture remains trapped under a strong inversion. Could even see \nsome rumbles of thunder given some weak elevated instability above \nthat inversion. Elevated dewpoints will also lead to mild lows, in \nthe upper 50s and low 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drying out Wednesday and especially Thursday under \nhigh pressure.  \n\nWednesday things begin to dry out behind the cold front and the \ninversion mixes out allowing for a mix of clouds and sun. However, a \nstronger reinforcing cold front brings a much cooler and drier \nairmass for Thursday as high pressure arrives. 925mb temps drop to \nnear 6C leading to highs only in the low 60s under mostly sunny \nskies. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Next storm system arrives Friday into Saturday. \n\nOur next storm, this one likely stronger than the mid week system, \noriginates out west Thursday. A low forms in the lee of the Rockies, \nphasing with another low over the Great Lakes as it is swept east by \na northern stream trough. It then strengthens as it moves over or \nnear New England Friday into Saturday. This pulls a lot of moisture \ninto the region and with good dynamics including potential for a \nstrong 45-55kt LLJ and favorable placement beneath the RRQ of a \n160kt upper jet, we should see a windy and very rainy Friday-\nSaturday. Timing and other details are uncertain at this time, but \nconfidence in at least a decently strong weather system is \nincreasing.",
